WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.960
Is this the roof of a majestic Beirut mosque?

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.400
No, it’s a Cheesecake Factory in Peoria, Arizona.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.900
Is this a fifteenth-century Italian beach house?

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:19.980
No, it’s the entrance to an epicurean world of&nbsp;
fine dining, courtesy of the Cheesecake Factory.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:28.260
And is this the Eye of Sauron, or perhaps some&nbsp;
portal to another dimension? Not exactly, though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:33.000
there is a transcendental quality to the Chocolate&nbsp;
Caramelicious Cheesecake Made With Snickers.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:39.360
Some describe these establishments&nbsp;
as excessive, pretentious, tacky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:43.080
tasteless, chaotic, soulless,&nbsp;
obnoxious, and overwhelming.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:46.740
But the Cheesecake Factory is misunderstood.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:55.020
Those giant Egyptian columns conceal complexity,&nbsp;
authenticity, and, oddly enough, humility.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:01.140
Sponsored by Brilliant (and&nbsp;
Brilliant only). Learn math,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:05.280
science, and computer science the intuitive&nbsp;
way with the link in the description.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:11.220
By size, the Cheesecake Factory&nbsp;
may not look all that impressive.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:18.180
With about 200 locations, it ranks only fifteenth&nbsp;
among American casual dining restaurants.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:24.960
There are seven and a half times as many&nbsp;
Applebee’s and four times as many Olive Gardens.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.760
But watch what happens when we&nbsp;
compare revenue per location.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.740
Suddenly, the Cheesecake Factory&nbsp;
sits in a league of its own.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:41.520
Its average restaurant generates 11&nbsp;
million dollars a year — 76% more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.260
than second-place Texas Roadhouse&nbsp;
and 60% more than P.F. Chang’s.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:52.500
Like In-N-Outs, they may be relatively&nbsp;
few and far between — in fact, there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:58.320
fewer Cheesecake Factories than In-N-Outs — but&nbsp;
each one is a certified money-printing machine.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:02.400
Put differently: that’s eleven hundred dollars per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:07.740
square foot — more than Kay Jewelers&nbsp;
made selling wedding rings in 2014.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:13.380
Avocado eggrolls are apparently a better&nbsp;
route to riches than literal diamonds.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.120
There are two main ingredients&nbsp;
to this incredible success.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:23.340
First, this is what demand looks&nbsp;
like at a typical restaurant over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:26.760
the course of the day, from Chili’s to Chipotle.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:34.740
During lunch and dinner, customers are easy to&nbsp;
find. In fact, you don’t find them. They find you.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:40.680
This, on the other hand — the&nbsp;
period between about 2 and 5pm — is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:45.120
every restaurant’s nightmare. Three&nbsp;
hours during the middle of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:48.840
every day, that are almost as&nbsp;
unprofitable as 8 in the morning.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.480
During this window, location is&nbsp;
extremely important. Customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.840
will stop by, but only if it’s convenient.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:04.020
The solution? Many Cheesecake&nbsp;
Factories are located in or near malls.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:10.020
Afternoon shoppers guarantee a steady flow&nbsp;
of foot traffic during this lull — solving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:14.760
one of the greatest challenges in the&nbsp;
industry — sharp fluctuations in demand.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.860
The other ingredient, of&nbsp;
course, is its legendary menu.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:25.320
What started as one page in the&nbsp;
1970s has now grown to twenty-one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:33.600
with 13 categories, 26 sub-categories,&nbsp;
and — wait for it — 250 unique items.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:39.180
What you’re looking at here is a condensed&nbsp;
version, without the descriptions, prices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:44.520
international translations, company story,&nbsp;
disclaimers, trademark symbols, and, yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:48.540
full-page cheesecake advertisements&nbsp;
usually scattered throughout.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:52.020
Neither does what you’re seeing&nbsp;
include the separate kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:56.580
happy hour, gluten free, or quote&nbsp;
“low calorie” “SkinnyLicious” menus.

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:01.680
At 6,000 words and with an estimated&nbsp;
reading time of 30 minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.800
the real menu is more treatise than Tweet.

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:08.700
From here, opinions quickly diverge.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:13.920
A deep, though unspoken schism&nbsp;
divides the cheesecake discourse.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:18.720
After extensive ethnographic&nbsp;
research and a few clogged arteries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.600
I’ve identified two customer archetypes.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:27.000
The first sees in the “darn-good”&nbsp;
3,000-calorie “Factory Nachos with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:33.420
Spicy Chicken” incredible value — a hearty dinner&nbsp;
and lunch the next day — all from an appetizer.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:40.320
From Italian to Mexican, Chinese, and American&nbsp;
cuisine, there’s truly something for everyone.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:46.680
Where else can grandma order a Cobb salad&nbsp;
and grandson a “cheeseburger spring roll”?

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:51.360
The other half sees a heart&nbsp;
attack waiting to happen.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:57.840
In this strange alternate universe, even a salad&nbsp;
can set you back nineteen hundred calories.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:04.320
The average American is advised to eat no&nbsp;
more than 20 grams of saturated fat per day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:10.680
The “Classic Basque Cheesecake” contains&nbsp;
88 — nearly four and a half days worth.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:15.360
And if all those calories don’t&nbsp;
cause a heart attack, they argue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:18.540
the architectural hyperstimulation will.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:23.820
The gigantic portions. The&nbsp;
21-page menu. The collision of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:28.740
Egyptian columns and Californian&nbsp;
palms. It’s all just… too much.

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:32.580
To this group, the Cheesecake Factory represents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:38.100
not just gross opulence but the only&nbsp;
thing worse: unjustified opulence.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:44.340
They imagine the company as three Olive&nbsp;
Gardens in a trench coat — a transparent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:49.080
clumsy attempt to make a corporate chain&nbsp;
appear more sophisticated than it clearly is.

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:55.200
They enjoy the same Very Cherry Ghirardelli®&nbsp;
Chocolate Cheesecake …but with a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:00.600
hesitation, from a psychological distance,&nbsp;
and with a tinge, perhaps, of smugness.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:06.480
There’s simply no way all two-hundred and&nbsp;
fifty dishes were meticulously crafted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:13.260
nevermind freshly prepared. Therefore, the&nbsp;
large menu must indicate a lack of taste,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:16.680
a cynical emphasis on quantity over quality.

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:24.120
…Except, here’s the thing: they were meticulously&nbsp;
crafted. And they are freshly prepared.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:30.600
Somewhere between restaurants 1 and&nbsp;
200, likely after going public in 1992,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:35.640
the Cheesecake Factory should have succumbed&nbsp;
to the natural process of ‘corporatization’.

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:41.160
It should have shed its quirky SoCal&nbsp;
personality and joined the homogenous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:44.820
soul-less heap of Olive&nbsp;
Gardens and Rainforest Cafes.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:51.600
These corporate ghosts may retain superficial&nbsp;
vestiges of their former glory — Olive Garden’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:57.540
shallow commitment to “hospitaliano” — but&nbsp;
inside they’ve become generic, cheap imitations.

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:04.680
Would you like your mass-produced, microwaved soup&nbsp;
served with a French beret or a Mexican sombrero?

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:08.460
Submerged in this sea of same-y brands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:12.480
who can blame these critics for assuming&nbsp;
the same of the Cheesecake Factory?

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:16.440
The company doesn’t scold those who scoff in its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:20.340
direction. Nor does it do much&nbsp;
to advertise its authenticity.

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:22.800
If anything, the opposite.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:28.740
You can certainly be forgiven for thinking food&nbsp;
from a self-described “factory” was mass-produced!

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:32.700
Shockingly, however, it’s really not.

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:39.180
Everything you see is made from scratch.&nbsp;
Not “scratch” with a wink. Really.

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:47.760
Starting at 6am each morning, chefs begin prepping&nbsp;
700 ingredients and 160 sauces, marinating each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:52.140
piece of buffalo chicken, shredding each&nbsp;
block of cheese, and chopping each tomato.

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:58.200
And because everything is made to order, the&nbsp;
company will gladly accommodate all kinds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:03.540
customizations. Try ordering a Caesar&nbsp;
Salad without lettuce and be amazed.

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:08.520
The only exception is the cheesecake&nbsp;
itself, which is made in one of two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:14.280
bakeries — in California and North Carolina&nbsp;
— and flown as far as Kuwait and China.

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:19.500
Now, does its hilariously large&nbsp;
menu allow the company to attract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:22.440
more customers and thus generate more profit?

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:25.560
Of course! Nobody is claiming otherwise.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:31.380
But every last one one of&nbsp;
those 250 meals are delicious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:35.520
And they aren’t chosen by focus&nbsp;
groups or in corporate boardrooms.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:41.100
Instead, the founder, law school drop-out,&nbsp;
and former rock musician David Overton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:44.880
now 76 years old, personally approves each one.

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:50.820
Other companies pay lip service to inclusivity&nbsp;
with half-hearted low-calorie, gluten-free,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:54.540
and vegetarian choices, only to&nbsp;
cut them when costs increase.

00:08:55.620 --> 00:09:00.780
After all, rule number one when it&nbsp;
comes to profitability is: less is more.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:06.720
Fewer options equals less complexity,&nbsp;
lower costs, and faster training.

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:12.480
In 2021, 60% of restaurants reported&nbsp;
reducing the size of their menus.

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:18.180
IHOP recently dropped a whole ten&nbsp;
pages. Dave &amp; Busters went from 40&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:22.260
items to 15. Even McDonald’s&nbsp;
switched to a “limited menu”.

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:29.220
The Cheesecake Factory, on the other hand,&nbsp;
maintains its mind-boggling cornucopia of 250&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:34.860
choices, replacing but not removing a dozen&nbsp;
or so every six months to keep things fresh.

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:41.160
A few are practically sacred — like “Evelyn's&nbsp;
Favorite Pasta”, named after David’s mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:45.240
and the “Original Cheesecake”&nbsp;
— unchanged since the 1940s.

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:51.000
Gaze into the surreal, dreamlike&nbsp;
abyss that is the Cheesecake Factory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:56.220
ponder its dizzying success,&nbsp;
and tell me: what do you see?

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:02.460
If the image staring back at you is that of an&nbsp;
insecure teenager sporting a briefcase and an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:07.980
oversized suit, it’s only because the illusion&nbsp;
of carelessness was so seamlessly executed.

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:12.000
The Cheesecake Factory is&nbsp;
so confident in its taste,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:16.140
freshness, and quality that it&nbsp;
rocks the oversized suit with ease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:22.560
It willingly, unnecessarily chooses to&nbsp;
expose itself to ridicule for our enjoyment.

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:28.680
It’s not a careless, chaotic, corporate&nbsp;
commodity masquerading as fine-dining.

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:34.380
It’s fine dining masquerading as a&nbsp;
careless, chaotic, corporate commodity.

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:38.280
Tragically, this modesty&nbsp;
has robbed it of its place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:41.280
in the small pantheon of lovable corporations.

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:46.380
Now, is the Cheesecake Factory weird? Objectively.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:51.000
But its weirdness is a product&nbsp;
of its role: a melting pot.

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:57.360
It lies at the intersection of blue and&nbsp;
white-collar America — a nice dinner out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:02.460
for the working class, and a fun — if slightly&nbsp;
ironic — indulgence for the middle class.

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:09.840
Its low-calorie, gluten-free, and over forty&nbsp;
vegetarian choices scream “come one, come all”.

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:15.600
And its strange palate is the result of an&nbsp;
indiscriminate fusion of cuisines — from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:18.660
“Tex Mex Eggrolls” to “Cheeseburger Spring Rolls”.

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:23.400
Finally, the Cheesecake Factory&nbsp;
is the ultimate American export.

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:28.380
Forget about the Statue of&nbsp;
Liberty or $20 million F-16s.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:36.600
85 countries and territories host American&nbsp;
military bases. 19 are “Major non-NATO allies”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:41.880
But only the most privileged — a&nbsp;
small, elite circle of ten — have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:46.620
the honor and distinction of hosting&nbsp;
a Cheesecake Factory — Canada, Mexico,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:54.000
Bahrain, Kuwait, Qatar, Saudi Arabia,&nbsp;
the UAE, China, Hong Kong, and Macau.

00:11:54.780 --> 00:12:00.120
These culinary outposts — shining beacons&nbsp;
of American decadence, multiculturalism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:06.840
and abundance — are as effective an ambassador&nbsp;
as any of its embassies, movies, or pop songs.

